/* ============================================================================
matrix-inverse-2x2.epher -- the 2x2 inverse by formula, A A^-1 = I

For A = [[a, b], [c, d]] with det D = ad - bc nonzero, the inverse is
(1/D) [[d, -b], [-c, a]] (any linear algebra text, e.g. Strang,
"Introduction to Linear Algebra", 5th ed., sec. 2.5). The classroom
formula is built by hand below, then A times it is checked to be the
identity (exactly, since the arithmetic is rational), and the built-in
inv is compared entry by entry. A = [[4, 7], [2, 6]] has det 10, so the
inverse entries are the terminating decimals 0.6, 0.7, 0.2, 0.4.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/algebra/matrices/matrix-inverse-2x2.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\algebra\matrices\matrix-inverse-2x2.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/algebra/matrices/matrix-inverse-2x2.epher

The knob to edit is the matrix A below.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob ----------------------------------------------------------------
const A = [[4, 7], [2, 6]]

// ---- helpers -----------------------------------------------------------------
def det2(m) = m[1][1] * m[2][2] - m[1][2] * m[2][1]                    // det of a 2x2
def invf(m) = (1 / det2(m)) * [[m[2][2], -m[1][2]], [-m[2][1], m[1][1]]]   // classroom formula
def same2(m, n) = abs(m[1][1] - n[1][1]) + abs(m[1][2] - n[1][2]) + abs(m[2][1] - n[2][1]) + abs(m[2][2] - n[2][2])

// ---- results -----------------------------------------------------------------
print("det:", det2(A))
print("inverse by formula:", invf(A))
print("A times its inverse:", A * invf(A))
print("builtin inv agrees:", same2(invf(A), inv(A)) < 1e-9)
/* ---- expected output ----
= [[4, 7], [2, 6]]
= det: 10
= inverse by formula: [[0.6, -0.7], [-0.2, 0.4]]
= A times its inverse: [[1, 0], [0, 1]]
= builtin inv agrees: true
*/
